  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They had two runs in the first and didn't score again until the eighth.",
    "what_this_means": "Drew Gilbert carried the offense with a home run and two RBI, but that wasn't enough when Cesar Perdomo lasted only 4.2 innings and San Diego kept answering. The bullpen held. Kuhnel, Sanmartin, Foley, Smith, and Seymour combining for 4.1 clean innings. But the lead evaporated early and stayed gone. Drew Cavanaugh sparked the eighth with a three-run shot to tie it, a moment that should have felt like momentum. It didn't. They lost 7-6 because one moment at the plate cannot carry a whole game when everything else goes sideways. The starter failed. The offense went silent for six innings. One rally isn't a strategy. It's hope dressed up as a comeback.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is a fragile team getting exposed by its own inconsistency. They led early, fell behind by four, and never had enough to finish what they started. Gilbert's work was real; Cavanaugh's eighth-inning at-bat mattered. But the game belonged to whoever held the lead in the fourth, and that wasn't them. They've lost their last two, and this one cuts deeper because they had the moments. The problem isn't firepower. It's that one solid hitter and some late innings don't defeat 4.2 innings of starter trouble and a six-inning offensive blackout. Fragile teams lose games like this by making heroes necessary instead of making outs impossible.",
